<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jayadhwaj Singha</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1648">1648</a>–<a href="/years/1663">1663</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Sutamla</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jayadhwaj Singha was king of the Ahom kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> from 1648 to 1663 and the first Ahom king to take initiation from a Vaishnavite satra. In 1662 the Mughal governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, Mir Jumla, invaded, occupied the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> and forced him into the hills, and in 1663 he accepted the harsh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghilajharighat">Treaty of Ghilajharighat</a>, which imposed tribute and the loss of western territory. He died soon afterwards.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jayadhwaj-singha.jpg" alt="Jayadhwaj Singha" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ComparingQuantities · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jayadhwaj_Singha_with_Banamali_dev.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Prince Chao-Sham, he was the son of the Ahom king Sutingpha, and his maternal uncle was Lecham Burhagohain of the Kuoigayan family. When the Burhagohain deposed his father, the prince took the throne with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> name Jayadhwaj Singha and the Tai name Sutamla; his coronation ceremony, the Singarigharutha, took place in April 1648. During his reign the Auniati and Dakhinpat satras were founded; he accepted initiation from Niranjan Bapu, made him head of the Auniati Satra and exempted the satra's disciples from labour owed to the state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> fell ill in 1658, Pran Narayan of Koch Bihar threw off Mughal rule and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kamrup">Kamrup</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajo">Hajo</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> pushed west as far as the Sankosh river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mir-jumla-ii">Mir Jumla II</a>, made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, soon retook Koch Bihar and on 4 January 1662 opened his campaign against the Ahoms. He overran the fort of Jogighopa on the Manas river, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guwahati">Guwahati</a>, Simlagarh and Salagarh, and on 17 March entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a>, which the king had abandoned for Namrup. This flight earned him the name Bhagania Roja in the Buranjis.</p><p>An early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> made it hard for Mir Jumla to carry off his booty, and the rearguard under Atan Burhagohain harried the Mughal army with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> tactics until they fell back on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> and Mathurapur while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahom-people">Ahoms</a> recovered the rest of the kingdom. The king came down from Namrup to camp at Solaguri, but he had to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ghilajharighat">Treaty of Ghilajharighat</a>, under which he sent his daughter Ramani Gabharu, born to his junior consort Pakhari Gabharu, to the Mughal empire.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He is said to have longed to see his ruined capital, and the sight of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/garhgaon">Garhgaon</a> left him stricken with grief. Back at his camp at Bakata he fell ill with severe diabetes and died after nine days, on 16 November 1663. On his deathbed he urged his ministers and nobles to protect the people and to draw from the nation's bosom the spear of humiliation fixed there by the enemy, the Mogul.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sutamla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1662 the Mughal governor of Bengal, Mir Jumla, invaded, occupied the capital Garhgaon and forced <b>Jayadhwaj Singha</b> to accept the harsh Treaty of Ghilajharighat in 1663, but disease and monsoon floods ruined the Mughal army.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Military</a></li></ul></section>
